      "text": "Integration of foam removal in the biological step of the process has also some advantages: existing aeration infrastructure and process vol- umes can be used, potential foaming problems can be mitigated, and no organic carbon will be lost during aeration. A significantly lower PFAS removal was demonstrated for MBRsludge and CASsludge in this study compared with MBRout and CASout, respectively ( p < 0.05). This, however, should not be regarded as the expected removal in the continuous process. If PFAS is continuously removed from the soluble phase of sludge liquor, less PFAS will be sorbed to sludge and the final removal efficiencies may be higher. However, long-chain PFAS has a higher affinity both for sorption to sludge and for concentration in foam compared to short-chain PFAS. Which of these affinities is stronger will determine the final removal efficiency for a specific substance. It is therefore hard to predict the final PFAS distribution and this has to be investigated experimentally by continuous trials. As was demonstrated in trials with activated sludge, both PFOA and PFAS can desorb from sludge when their concentration in the liquid phase is lowered, although the desorption of PFOA was found to be more profound. The desorption of PFDA was minimal leading to negligible removal efficiency. In a recent study, the enrichment of PFAS in the liquid and the solid fraction"
